Z||000000||jhar1987||03-08-2000||10:04 PM||||i keep getting a 35 code on my dis motor. The book tells me that the iac was bad, so i changed it. I set it to specs. per instructions and was doing good. After a couple of days i got the code again. I disconnected the battery and reset everything but this didn't help. Also my car now idles at 500rpm when its cold out and 1500 when its warm????? any ideas? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||mwbackus||03-08-2000||10:45 PM||||Vacuum leak.||||reg||1
Z||000002||jhar1987||03-08-2000||11:46 PM||||i changed all the vacuum hoses,anything else that could be causing it?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carolina 88GT||03-09-2000||07:17 AM||||I had a cracked o2 that gave me a code 35.  Replaced it with a Bosch and no problems.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||03-09-2000||11:14 AM||||coolant and/or manifold air temp could also cause idle problems.<P>note: if you must repair the wires/connectors to either sensor then the joint must be soldered. No other method is acceptable for these circuits. Bosch coolant temp sensor comes with a new plug. it will fit the manifold temp sensor as well. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||jhar1987||03-09-2000||11:40 PM||||i replaced the temp sensor and the manifold air sensor. could the factory sensor plugs be the problem? they appeared to be ok.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||03-10-2000||07:19 PM||||Yes they could. If those connectors are even a little damaged/worn/contaminated they can add resistance to the circuit and throw off the reading to the ECM.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||jhar1987||03-10-2000||09:28 PM||||orge can i buy new connectors for the sensors?||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||03-11-2000||11:22 AM||||Yes. Not sure of where. look at wrenchead and carparts .coms. look with the sensors, but dig around. sometimes they aren't obvious.<P>Borg warner coolant sensors come with one. Other don't come with it but need it so they can be used in place of the older version of the sensor. No one makes the old round connector sensor anymore that was used before 86-87. So those guys always have to replace the sensor connector whith the new style one.||||reg||1
Z||000009||mwbackus||03-11-2000||12:13 PM||||You can purchase nearly all of the sensor connectors from the Fiero Store (new product).  I would still attempt finding the vacuum leak, replacing lines does not fix leaks in TB or intake gaskets.  The fact that there are idle problems immediatly upon startup would indicate that it is not a sensor problem (ie.. open loop mode).  <P>Mike||||reg||1
